In article <19339@hydra.gatech.EDU> gt0924a@prism.gatech.EDU (DELGADO,MARK MARCH (Metallica, TheDeathAngel)) writes:
>Someone also mentioned making copies of level.* to get all kinds of goodies
>I assume that is legit with a wand of wishing also?
>
>and has anyone changed a level.* to a different level.*??

It was me who mentioned that.  Back when I first discovered this, in
PC Hack 1.0.1E (anyone here still remember that?) and later PC Hack
3.0, you could do almost *anything* with the levels.* files, including
trading them between different games/characters.
I created a 'supply level', a level.* file that I would dump all sorts
of useful magic, food, etc., in.  Whenever a character was in trouble
I could always 'swap in' the supply level, and save him.

I haven't read the source code since then, but it seems they've put
some sort of checksum or other trick in to keep you from doing such
outrageous stuff.  In NetHack 3.0.9, the only thing you can do with a
level file is to copy it into the same game it came from, and at the
same number.
Of course, in PC Hack I found other fun stuff to do with the game,
after I figured out the format of the save file and attacked it with
my binary editor.  I still have PC Hack 3.0 around just so I can
occasionally play my 255th level Wizard with his +99 two handed sword
and his multitude of wands of wishing.  I even changed him to look
like a radiant star instead of an @ sign.
After a while, though, it just isn't hack anymore.  It's figuring
stuff out and trying it that's fun, but once you've got it, it's a lot
more fun just to go back to playing real games of NetHack.

